  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nLarge and energetic trough and upper low to the northeast will \ncontinue to move east with time today. Heights rise some in its \nwake, with cool high pressure sinking over northern Michigan, \nespecially later this afternoon and evening. Thus, temperatures \ngenerally in the mid to upper 50s across much of the area with low \n60s near Saginaw Bay. Northwest winds remain on the slightly breezy \nside today, gusts 15 to 25 mph at times. As high pressure oozes over \nnorthern Michigan this evening, winds will decrease and the \natmosphere will decouple, resulting in efficient radiational \ncooling. Overnight low temps will drop into the 30s almost \neverywhere, near or below freezing across portions of the interior. \nHigh pressure slides to the east late during the overnight hours in \nresponse to the next piece of energy aloft and low pressure \nsystem in the Dakotas. This will likely introduce a little \nsouthern flow late tonight, outside of the sheltered areas and \nvalleys, along with increasing cloud cover, especially across \nnorthwest lower Michigan. That being said, current forecast does\nsuggest frost/freeze across a good chunk of interior northern \nlower Michigan. \n\nWarm advection begins overspreading northern MI on Tuesday as\nwinds shift to the south and southwest. Consequently,\ntemperatures will be on the increase with most locations back\ninto the 60s. Low pressure system across the Dakotas also\nmarches eastward into Ontario, with the best potential for\nprecipitation during the evening/early overnight hours with the\nbetter forcing and \"slightly\" deeper moisture. This will be\nespecially true across the eastern UP & vicinity. The more \nnoticeable feature will be the increasing winds, especially for \nnorthwest lower MI and out over the nearshore waters. Warm \nadvection and the potential for a slightly messy boundary layer\nmay limit some wind production, but it will be breezy to windy\nday nevertheless, whether on land or across the waters of Lake\nMichigan.\n\nThe rest of the work week will feature breezy conditions and on/off \nshower chances as energy scrapes the northern portions of the region \nand the lakes respond accordingly to the cooler temperatures aloft.\nTemperatures will fluctuate from milder on Wednesday (mid 60s) to a \nlittle cooler late week with overnight lows dipping back into the \n30s for some. \n\nRising heights this weekend will result in increasing temperatures, \nespecially the second half of the weekend (70s on Sunday?). \nPrecipitation chances during this time appear to be low.",
